WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.088
This is an implementation guide.

2
00:00:02.088 --> 00:00:07.215
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
07 decided that the program's certificate

3
00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:10.465
lifecycle platform is
CyberArk Certificate Manager,

4
00:00:10.465 --> 00:00:14.149
from the vendor the agency
already uses for machine identity.

5
00:00:14.149 --> 00:00:17.366
This guide shows how to
build it, self-hosted,

6
00:00:17.366 --> 00:00:20.850
and connect it to Book 07's
design, phase by phase.

7
00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.062
It runs in four parts
after a short orientation.

8
00:00:24.062 --> 00:00:27.188
Part 1 builds the platform: architecture,

9
00:00:27.188 --> 00:00:31.325
the FIPS posture, console
sign-in, hardening, and backup.

10
00:00:31.325 --> 00:00:35.804
Part 2 connects the issuers: Active
Directory Certificate Services,

11
00:00:35.804 --> 00:00:39.575
the public certificate authorities,
the cloud, and Intune.

12
00:00:39.575 --> 00:00:44.013
Part 3 covers discovery, ownership
and enrollment automation.

13
00:00:44.013 --> 00:00:50.063
And Part 4 maps the build onto Book 07's
six migration waves and covers running it.

14
00:00:50.063 --> 00:00:51.374
Three ground rules.

15
00:00:51.374 --> 00:00:56.895
Every fact comes from a primary source
opened on October twenty-second,

16
00:00:56.895 --> 00:01:00.825
2026, mostly CyberArk's
version 26.1 documentation.

17
00:01:00.825 --> 00:01:03.738
Recommendations are
labeled design guidance.

18
00:01:03.738 --> 00:01:08.382
And in three places the research
corrects Book 07; I'll state the

19
00:01:08.382 --> 00:01:13.562
corrected fact each time, and Book 07
has been updated to match to match.

20
00:01:13.562 --> 00:01:19.736
Sources: Research brief,
Implementing Book 07's certificate

21
00:01:19.736 --> 00:01:26.898
lifecycle on CyberArk Certificate
Manager, Self-Hosted (verified Oct.

22
00:01:26.898 --> 00:01:33.690
22, 2026); CyberArk Trust Protection
Foundation 26.1 documentation;

23
00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.380
Book 07, Chapters 01, 04, 05, 07 and 08.

24
00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:43.258
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
07's target gives each class of subject

25
00:01:43.258 --> 00:01:46.199
its own issuer, and a
lifecycle platform covers

26
00:01:46.199 --> 00:01:49.442
the issuers that don't renew
their own certificates.

27
00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:54.942
Intune-managed devices get user and device
certificates from Microsoft Cloud PKI

28
00:01:54.942 --> 00:02:00.281
through SCEP, or during the bridge period
from AD CS through the Intune connector.

29
00:02:00.281 --> 00:02:04.342
Intune renews them, so the CLM
platform doesn't manage them.

30
00:02:04.342 --> 00:02:07.368
PIV cards come from a
Federal PKI shared service

31
00:02:07.368 --> 00:02:10.242
provider through the
card management system.

32
00:02:10.242 --> 00:02:15.008
Domain controller, KDC and LDAPS
certificates stay on retained AD CS

33
00:02:15.008 --> 00:02:19.856
with autoenrollment; the platform
watches them but never renews them.

34
00:02:19.856 --> 00:02:22.067
Everything else lands on the platform.

35
00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:26.551
RADIUS and NPS servers, VPN and
Remote Desktop gateways, SQL Server,

36
00:02:26.551 --> 00:02:31.850
internal web servers and appliances get
certificates from the retained AD CS

37
00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:36.253
hierarchy, which becomes a standalone
CA after Active Directory,

38
00:02:36.253 --> 00:02:40.492
requested and renewed through
CyberArk Certificate Manager.

39
00:02:40.492 --> 00:02:46.127
Public-facing TLS comes from a public CA
the agency authorizes in its CAA records,

40
00:02:46.127 --> 00:02:50.668
renewed by the platform, with ACME
where the endpoint supports it.

41
00:02:50.668 --> 00:02:52.672
And for the hierarchy itself,

42
00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:57.431
the platform's revocation monitoring
checks CRL and OCSP endpoints.

43
00:02:57.431 --> 00:03:01.156
So the platform's job is simple
to state: it's the system of

44
00:03:01.156 --> 00:03:05.692
record for every certificate Intune
and the PIV system don't manage.

45
00:03:05.692 --> 00:03:11.677
Sources: Book 07, Chapter 04 (issuer
map; certificate lifecycle management),

46
00:03:11.677 --> 00:03:18.251
Chapter 05 (renewal ownership per certificate
class), Chapter 08 (renewal engines);

47
00:03:18.251 --> 00:03:22.667
research brief section 10;
CyberArk 26.1 Installation

48
00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:27.530
and Upgrade Guide (Certificate
Revocation Monitoring).

49
00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:29.455
Why self-hosted?

50
00:03:29.455 --> 00:03:33.102
In the FedRAMP Marketplace data
as of October twenty-second,

51
00:03:33.102 --> 00:03:37.187
there's no certificate-management
package from Palo Alto Networks,

52
00:03:37.187 --> 00:03:39.885
CyberArk or Venafi: not
Certificate Manager,

53
00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:44.043
not Next-Gen Trust Security, and
not the Trust Protection Foundation.

54
00:03:44.043 --> 00:03:47.584
Palo Alto's authorized packages
cover privileged access,

55
00:03:47.584 --> 00:03:50.904
endpoint privilege management
and network security.

56
00:03:50.904 --> 00:03:55.475
So the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 07's decision stands:

57
00:03:55.475 --> 00:03:59.380
run Certificate Manager,
Self-Hosted inside the agency's

58
00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:03.368
own authorization boundary,
alongside the CAs it manages.

59
00:04:03.368 --> 00:04:06.327
The program's design
guidance adds a corollary:

60
00:04:06.327 --> 00:04:10.956
avoid any feature that depends on the
SaaS, and we'll meet two of those later.

61
00:04:10.956 --> 00:04:14.019
The product runs on the
Trust Protection Foundation,

62
00:04:14.019 --> 00:04:16.805
formerly the Venafi Trust
Protection Platform.

63
00:04:16.805 --> 00:04:22.237
The current release is 26.1, from July
2026, and CyberArk typically ships

64
00:04:22.237 --> 00:04:27.668
major releases every six months, so
plan an upgrade cadence from day one.

65
00:04:27.668 --> 00:04:33.115
The servers run Windows Server 2016
through 2025, with the desktop experience,

66
00:04:33.115 --> 00:04:38.730
from English installation media only, and
the same OS version across the cluster.

67
00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:45.105
They need IIS, .NET 4.8, the
Visual C++ runtimes and WebView2.

68
00:04:45.105 --> 00:04:48.800
The database is SQL
Server 2014 through 2022,

69
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.793
also English only, and not on a
platform server except in test.

70
00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:58.704
CyberArk strongly recommends the FULL
recovery model, and recommends Enterprise

71
00:04:58.704 --> 00:05:02.905
edition because Standard needs an
outage for the weekly index rebuild.

72
00:05:02.905 --> 00:05:06.693
For high availability, use
Always On availability groups.

73
00:05:06.693 --> 00:05:11.885
Sources: FedRAMP Marketplace
products.json (last_change Oct.

74
00:05:11.885 --> 00:05:18.011
22, 2026); CyberArk 26.1 System
Requirements, Always On high availability,

75
00:05:18.011 --> 00:05:24.241
Choosing SQL Server Enterprise or
Standard, Important Considerations,

76
00:05:24.241 --> 00:05:27.044
Installation and Upgrade Guide;

77
00:05:27.044 --> 00:05:33.110
