Security page; Book 07, Chapter 04.

78
00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:35.810
Part 1: build the platform.

79
00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.538
We'll start with the
architecture: the cluster,

80
00:05:38.538 --> 00:05:41.985
its components, how to size it
and make it highly available.

81
00:05:41.985 --> 00:05:46.073
Then the FIPS posture, where you have
to be careful about what you claim.

82
00:05:46.073 --> 00:05:48.884
Then how administrators
sign in to the console.

83
00:05:48.884 --> 00:05:52.446
And finally hardening,
least-privilege service accounts,

84
00:05:52.446 --> 00:05:55.710
and backup and recovery for
the database and its keys.

85
00:05:55.710 --> 00:06:01.609
Sources: CyberArk 26.1 System Requirements,
Installation and Upgrade Guide,

86
00:06:01.609 --> 00:06:06.946
security hardening and disaster
recovery documentation; NIST CMVP;

87
00:06:06.946 --> 00:06:11.720
the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 07, Chapter 05.

88
00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:13.645
The architecture.

89
00:06:13.645 --> 00:06:20.298
Several Trust Protection Foundation servers
sharing one SQL database form a cluster,

90
00:06:20.298 --> 00:06:25.195
and a message bus, an MQTT broker
using TLS on port 8883, links them.

91
00:06:25.195 --> 00:06:29.807
Each server runs the components you switch
on for it in the Configuration Console.

92
00:06:29.807 --> 00:06:34.994
The ones this guide uses are CA Import,
Certificate Lifecycle and Monitoring,

93
00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:39.857
Certificate Revocation Monitoring,
Network Discovery, the EST service,

94
00:06:39.857 --> 00:06:44.395
Network Device Enrollment for SCEP,
the ACME service and Reporting.

95
00:06:44.395 --> 00:06:49.693
You can keep work on particular servers
with processing engines, discovery zones,

96
00:06:49.693 --> 00:06:53.043
or by turning components
off, and that matters later,

97
00:06:53.043 --> 00:06:55.770
when we keep ACME off
the FIPS-mode servers.

98
00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.208
Sizing comes from CyberArk's tables.

99
00:06:58.208 --> 00:07:01.896
Two servers handle up to fifty
thousand active certificates.

100
00:07:01.896 --> 00:07:06.850
Up to two hundred fifty thousand takes
six: two for log and event processing,

101
00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:09.807
two for web services and
two back-end servers.

102
00:07:09.807 --> 00:07:14.808
Past a million, CyberArk designs it with
you, and SQL Enterprise is required.

103
00:07:14.808 --> 00:07:16.758
Each server needs four cores,

104
00:07:16.758 --> 00:07:20.496
sixteen gigabytes of memory
and five gigabytes of disk.

105
00:07:20.496 --> 00:07:25.082
For availability: Always On for the
database, at least two servers per role,

106
00:07:25.082 --> 00:07:30.045
and a load balancer in front of the web
services, which rolling upgrades require.

107
00:07:30.045 --> 00:07:35.251
For the CAs, a Microsoft CA Pool holds
up to three CA templates; if the first

108
00:07:35.251 --> 00:07:40.457
CA fails, the platform stops querying
it for sixty minutes and fails over.

109
00:07:40.457 --> 00:07:45.920
Sources: CyberArk 26.1 System
Requirements (cluster, Message Bus,

110
00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:51.486
sizing, ports, network scoping);
Installation and Upgrade Guide pp.

111
00:07:51.486 --> 00:07:54.991
54 and 86–94 (components);
Rolling upgrades;

112
00:07:54.991 --> 00:08:01.320
