230
00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:01.903
Part 2: connect the issuers.

231
00:16:01.903 --> 00:16:04.905
First the AD CS driver:
what it needs on the CA,

232
00:16:04.905 --> 00:16:09.577
how templates map, and which Windows
Server versions are confirmed.

233
00:16:09.577 --> 00:16:13.488
Then how to scope its privileged
account under the ESC7 review,

234
00:16:13.488 --> 00:16:18.827
which includes the first correction to the
Federal Modernization Workbook's Book 07.

235
00:16:18.827 --> 00:16:24.327
Then the public and cloud CA drivers,
where release 26.1 changed one.

236
00:16:24.327 --> 00:16:29.502
And finally how the platform coexists with
Intune, which owns device certificates.

237
00:16:29.502 --> 00:16:36.382
Sources: CyberArk 26.1 CA driver
documentation, Supported integrations,

238
00:16:36.382 --> 00:16:40.510
Deprecated features;
Microsoft Learn AD CS,

239
00:16:40.510 --> 00:16:46.380
Intune and Microsoft Graph pages;
Book 07, Chapters 03 to 07.

240
00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:49.043
The AD CS driver.

241
00:16:49.043 --> 00:16:52.596
In the platform it's a CA
template object called Microsoft

242
00:16:52.596 --> 00:16:56.817
Active Directory Certificate
Services, Enterprise and Standalone.

243
00:16:56.817 --> 00:17:00.962
It needs Microsoft CA Support
installed on the platform servers,

244
00:17:00.962 --> 00:17:02.880
and it talks to the CA over DCOM.

245
00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:06.817
The account it uses needs Read,
Issue and Manage Certificates,

246
00:17:06.817 --> 00:17:09.055
and Request Certificates on the CA.

247
00:17:09.055 --> 00:17:13.005
That matches the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 07.

248
00:17:13.005 --> 00:17:15.380
Template mapping is done by policy.

249
00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:19.718
A certificate gets its CA template
object from the policy on its folder,

250
00:17:19.718 --> 00:17:21.630
or directly on the certificate.

251
00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:25.588
CyberArk's vCert tool expects the
template to come from policy and

252
00:17:25.588 --> 00:17:29.617
calls choosing it per request
against CyberArk's recommendations.

253
00:17:29.617 --> 00:17:33.453
So, by inference, the natural
pattern is one CyberArk CA

254
00:17:33.453 --> 00:17:38.056
template object for each AD CS
template the platform issues from.

255
00:17:38.056 --> 00:17:41.280
Enterprise and standalone
CAs are both supported.

256
00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:44.812
Only enterprise CAs use
Active Directory templates,

257
00:17:44.812 --> 00:17:49.387
which is exactly why Book 07 moves
server issuance to a standalone CA

258
00:17:49.387 --> 00:17:54.042
after the forest retires, with template
policy living in the platform.

259
00:17:54.042 --> 00:17:59.455
Letting requesters set end dates
may need a CA policy flag turned on;

260
00:17:59.455 --> 00:18:03.223
that raises event 4892
under Book 07 Chapter 03,

261
00:18:03.223 --> 00:18:08.055
so the program's guidance is to
leave it off unless you need it.

262
00:18:08.055 --> 00:18:09.117
One open item.

263
00:18:09.117 --> 00:18:12.763
The driver page says it
supports CAs on all supported

264
00:18:12.763 --> 00:18:16.492
Windows Server versions, and
the platform runs on 2025.

265
00:18:16.492 --> 00:18:19.874
But CyberArk's
supported-integrations table

266
00:18:19.874 --> 00:18:23.167
lists AD CS only through
Windows Server 2022.

267
00:18:23.167 --> 00:18:29.780
If any retained CA runs Windows Server 2025,
get written confirmation before Wave 2.

268
00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:35.401
Sources: CyberArk 26.1 Microsoft ADCS
Enterprise and Standalone CA template;

269
00:18:35.401 --> 00:18:38.826
MSCA certificate object
settings; CA chaining;

270
00:18:38.826 --> 00:18:44.007
Configuring certificate import from
a Microsoft CA; Microsoft CA Pool;

271
00:18:44.007 --> 00:18:49.013
Supported integrations; Deprecated
features; System Requirements;

272
00:18:49.013 --> 00:18:53.860
vCert README; Microsoft Learn,
Manage certificate templates.

273
00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:55.997
Now the sensitive part.

274
00:18:55.997 --> 00:19:00.761
Issue and Manage Certificates is
the AD CS certificate-manager right.

275
00:19:00.761 --> 00:19:03.531
Microsoft says it lets
the holder issue, deny,

276
00:19:03.531 --> 00:19:08.398
revoke and reinstate certificates
without the broader Manage CA permission.

277
00:19:08.398 --> 00:19:13.786
Defender for Identity's ESC7 assessment
flags unprivileged holders of it.

278
00:19:13.786 --> 00:19:18.673
Here's the first correction to the
Federal Modernization Workbook's Book 07.

279
00:19:18.673 --> 00:19:22.882
Chapter 05 says the program scopes
the account to the templates

280
00:19:22.882 --> 00:19:27.011
the platform is meant to issue, as
if the CA had a control for that.

281
00:19:27.011 --> 00:19:32.257
Microsoft's current guidance says to define
each certificate manager's templates and

282
00:19:32.257 --> 00:19:36.265
requesters and to run positive
and negative authorization tests,

283
00:19:36.265 --> 00:19:41.220
but it also says Microsoft Learn doesn't
publish a Windows Server 2025 step-by-step

284
00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:45.083
procedure for configuring
certificate-manager restrictions,

285
00:19:45.083 --> 00:19:48.435
and to validate any existing
restriction in a lab first.

286
00:19:48.435 --> 00:19:51.155
So the scoping rests on
template permissions,

287
00:19:51.155 --> 00:19:54.448
plus allowed and denied test
requests proven in the lab.

288
00:19:54.448 --> 00:19:57.973
Book 07 has been updated
to match to say so.

289
00:19:57.973 --> 00:20:00.040
My inference about why that matters:

290
00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:03.348
template permissions limit
what the account can request.

291
00:20:03.348 --> 00:20:06.400
They don't narrow the
certificate-manager right itself,

292
00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:09.785
which is why the negative test
and the alerts carry the weight.

293
00:20:09.785 --> 00:20:12.760
The program's controls,
as design guidance.

294
00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:16.034
A dedicated account, a group
managed service account

295
00:20:16.034 --> 00:20:19.010
where the CA allows it,
denied interactive logon.

296
00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:23.110
DCOM to the CA allowed only from
the platform's engine servers.

297
00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:26.285
Read and Enroll only on
the platform's templates.

298
00:20:26.285 --> 00:20:31.992
Alerts on event 4887 when this
requester gets a certificate

299
00:20:31.992 --> 00:20:36.672
outside those templates,
and on 4882, 4890 and 4892.

300
00:20:36.672 --> 00:20:40.187
And the account goes into
the quarterly ESC7 review,

301
00:20:40.187 --> 00:20:42.885
with the negative test result on record.

302
00:20:42.885 --> 00:20:48.994
Sources: Microsoft Learn, Secure, delegate
and audit a certification authority;

303
