307
00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:11.595
The public and cloud CA drivers.

308
00:21:11.595 --> 00:21:16.804
For public TLS, 26.1 documents
drivers for DigiCert,

309
00:21:16.804 --> 00:21:22.757
Sectigo, GlobalSign, HID,
QuoVadis, VikingCloud and Amazon.

310
00:21:22.757 --> 00:21:26.182
Two 26.1 changes matter
before you upgrade.

311
00:21:26.182 --> 00:21:30.717
Entrust Certificate Services is no
longer supported as a standalone CA;

312
00:21:30.717 --> 00:21:35.781
CyberArk says those customers migrated
to Sectigo Certificate Manager, though

313
00:21:35.781 --> 00:21:40.770
the Entrust CA Gateway driver for Entrust's
own CA software is still documented.

314
00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:46.245
So if the agency's public CA is Entrust,
plan the issuing path before the upgrade.

315
00:21:46.245 --> 00:21:51.856
And for DigiCert client and S/MIME
certificates, an email subject alternative

316
00:21:51.856 --> 00:21:57.295
name is now required; automated renewals
without one fail after the upgrade.

317
00:21:57.295 --> 00:21:58.645
Note one absence.

318
00:21:58.645 --> 00:22:02.315
There's no documented ACME
client driver for public CAs,

319
00:22:02.315 --> 00:22:05.507
so no Let's Encrypt-style
path out to the internet.

320
00:22:05.507 --> 00:22:09.330
The platform is an ACME
server for your own clients,

321
00:22:09.330 --> 00:22:11.696
not an ACME client to public CAs.

322
00:22:11.696 --> 00:22:15.220
On the cloud side, I'm stating
only what's documented.

323
00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:21.047
The AWS Certificate Manager driver issues
public and private certificates and works

324
00:22:21.047 --> 00:22:26.308
with Amazon Private CA, and CA Import
can pull Private CA certificates through

325
00:22:26.308 --> 00:22:31.245
audit reports written to S3, which AWS
limits to once every thirty minutes.

326
00:22:31.245 --> 00:22:35.798
Azure Key Vault is a provisioning
target, not a CA: the platform stores

327
00:22:35.798 --> 00:22:40.032
certificates, keys and chains there
and can bind them to web apps.

328
00:22:40.032 --> 00:22:42.779
What I couldn't find is
any statement that the Key

329
00:22:42.779 --> 00:22:45.732
Vault driver supports
Azure Government endpoints.

330
00:22:45.732 --> 00:22:49.319
Test that, or ask CyberArk,
before you rely on it.

331
