368
00:25:20.850 --> 00:25:24.013
Part 3: discover, own and automate.

369
00:25:24.013 --> 00:25:29.350
First, how the platform finds certificates:
discovery scans and CA import.

370
00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:31.909
Then how each certificate gets an owner,

371
00:25:31.909 --> 00:25:35.475
through policy folders tied
to identity and to the CMDB.

372
00:25:35.475 --> 00:25:39.084
Then the enrollment protocols,
where the third correction

373
00:25:39.084 --> 00:25:42.325
to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 07 sits.

374
00:25:42.325 --> 00:25:45.523
And finally three
integrations: Kubernetes,

375
00:25:45.523 --> 00:25:49.325
ServiceNow and the SIEM, each
with a boundary question.

376
00:25:49.325 --> 00:25:56.301
Sources: CyberArk 26.1 discovery, policy,
permissions, ACME, EST, SCEP, OAuth,

377
00:25:56.301 --> 00:26:01.184
Kubernetes discovery and
logging documentation;

378
00:26:01.184 --> 00:26:05.140
vCert; cert-manager; ServiceNow Store.

379
00:26:05.890 --> 00:26:06.777
Discovery.

380
00:26:06.777 --> 00:26:10.842
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 07 Chapter 02

381
00:26:10.842 --> 00:26:15.078
points out that a CA database
shows only what that CA issued.

382
00:26:15.078 --> 00:26:19.956
Certificates from public CAs, appliances
or self-signing don't appear there,

383
00:26:19.956 --> 00:26:22.666
and finding them takes network discovery.

384
00:26:22.666 --> 00:26:24.615
The platform has five ways in.

385
00:26:24.615 --> 00:26:30.153
Network Discovery scans IPv4 ranges
and ports for TLS listeners,

386
00:26:30.153 --> 00:26:34.665
and can instantly check a
single IPv4 or IPv6 address.

387
00:26:34.665 --> 00:26:38.984
CA Import pulls what a Microsoft
CA or Amazon Private CA issued,

388
00:26:38.984 --> 00:26:44.465
template by template, optionally including
expired and revoked certificates.

389
00:26:44.465 --> 00:26:49.640
Onboard Discovery is agentless and brings
the application configuration with it.

390
00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:53.860
And the Discovery Import API takes
results from a collector you write,

391
00:26:53.860 --> 00:26:55.752
such as the Graph job from Part 2.

392
00:26:55.752 --> 00:26:58.478
Reconciliation decides
whether a discovered

393
00:26:58.478 --> 00:27:01.203
certificate is one the
platform already knows.

394
00:27:01.203 --> 00:27:06.177
It matches on subject, key usage, extended
key usage and the DNS names in the

395
00:27:06.177 --> 00:27:11.315
subject alternative name, and the newer
certificate becomes the active one.

396
00:27:11.315 --> 00:27:14.715
It doesn't apply to CA
Import or Onboard Discovery.

397
00:27:14.715 --> 00:27:19.547
The program's rule, from Book 07
Chapter 08: every certificate found

398
00:27:19.547 --> 00:27:24.027
on a listener with no issuance
record and no owner is a finding.

399
00:27:24.027 --> 00:27:28.257
As design guidance for Wave 1,
run read-only network scans of

400
00:27:28.257 --> 00:27:33.090
server and appliance ranges, and
import the in-scope AD CS templates.

401
00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:36.257
That import needs the
privileged CA account,

402
00:27:36.257 --> 00:27:39.252
so create it under the
ESC7 controls first.

403
00:27:39.252 --> 00:27:43.739
Sources: CyberArk 26.1
Certificate discovery overview;

404
00:27:43.739 --> 00:27:50.372
Configuring Network Discovery; Certificate
reconciliation; Onboard Discovery;

405
00:27:50.372 --> 00:27:56.907
Agent discovery; Importing certificates
from a CA; WebSDK POST Discovery/Import;

406
00:27:56.907 --> 00:28:01.940
