(SCIM 2.0); Vendor support for SSO;

434
00:29:53.026 --> 00:29:59.360
ServiceNow Store, Machine Identity
Protection; Book 07, Chapters 05 and 08.

435
00:30:00.110 --> 00:30:03.707
Enrollment automation, starting
with the third correction

436
00:30:03.707 --> 00:30:06.585
to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 07.

437
00:30:06.585 --> 00:30:12.761
Book 07 Chapter 08 says CyberArk documents
that the win-acme client needs FIPS mode

438
00:30:12.761 --> 00:30:18.847
off, and advises using another tested client
where the server must run in FIPS mode.

439
00:30:18.847 --> 00:30:22.923
The 26.1 Installation and
Upgrade Guide is broader.

440
00:30:22.923 --> 00:30:27.109
It says that because FIPS is
not supported for ACMEv2,

441
00:30:27.109 --> 00:30:31.485
you can't have FIPS enabled on
any server used for ACMEv2.

442
00:30:31.485 --> 00:30:34.189
The topic page still names only win-acme;

443
00:30:34.189 --> 00:30:38.123
the stricter text governs
until CyberArk says otherwise.

444
00:30:38.123 --> 00:30:43.821
The program's design guidance: run the ACME
service on dedicated servers that aren't

445
00:30:43.821 --> 00:30:48.860
in FIPS mode, kept out of the FIPS-mode
set, or get a written vendor statement.

446
00:30:48.860 --> 00:30:51.710
Book 07 has been updated to match.

447
00:30:51.710 --> 00:30:59.736
The ACME service itself implements RFC
8555 with http-01 and dns-01 challenges,

448
00:30:59.736 --> 00:31:05.910
and was designed and tested with
certbot, win-acme and acme.sh.

449
00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:10.647
The page both lists wildcard issuance
and calls it future work, so test it.

450
00:31:10.647 --> 00:31:16.883
EST runs on port 50443 with client-certificate
authentication, and a CA label in

451
00:31:16.883 --> 00:31:23.023
the URL selects the CA template; three
optional EST functions aren't supported.

452
00:31:23.023 --> 00:31:26.548
SCEP, for network devices, needs port 80.

453
00:31:26.548 --> 00:31:31.007
For everything else there's
the REST API with OAuth tokens,

454
00:31:31.007 --> 00:31:35.838
and CyberArk's vCert command-line
tool, currently version 5.13.9,

455
00:31:35.838 --> 00:31:39.647
which needs view, read,
write and create on its zone.

456
00:31:39.647 --> 00:31:43.495
Agentless drivers push
certificates to the endpoints,

457
00:31:43.495 --> 00:31:48.011
licensed per instance in 26.1, and
the Server Agent covers the rest.

458
00:31:48.011 --> 00:31:53.116
Sources: CyberArk 26.1
Installation and Upgrade Guide p.

459
00:31:53.116 --> 00:32:00.108
50; ACME overview; EST overview,
limitations and reference; Configuring SCEP;

460
00:32:00.108 --> 00:32:05.435
NDE rules; Setting up OAuth;
System Requirements; What's New;

461
00:32:05.435 --> 00:32:10.460
vCert README and latest
release; Book 07, Chapter 08.

462
00:32:11.210 --> 00:32:14.360
Three integrations, each
with a boundary question.

463
00:32:14.360 --> 00:32:15.447
Kubernetes.

464
00:32:15.447 --> 00:32:18.612
The platform has a Kubernetes
Discovery component,

465
00:32:18.612 --> 00:32:23.176
but it imports certificates from
clusters registered to the SaaS edition,

466
00:32:23.176 --> 00:32:26.047
and it needs outbound
HTTPS to the SaaS service.

467
00:32:26.047 --> 00:32:28.269
With no FedRAMP package for the SaaS,

468
00:32:28.269 --> 00:32:32.047
the program's guidance is not to
plan on it inside the boundary.

469
00:32:32.047 --> 00:32:34.460
The in-boundary path is cert-manager.

470
00:32:34.460 --> 00:32:38.082
Its CyberArk issuer,
formerly the Venafi issuer,

471
00:32:38.082 --> 00:32:41.160
talks directly to the self-hosted WebSDK.

472
00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:46.490
You configure one issuer per zone, with
an access token or username and password,

473
00:32:46.490 --> 00:32:50.447
and refresh tokens aren't
supported, so plan token rotation.

474
00:32:50.447 --> 00:32:51.460
ServiceNow.

475
00:32:51.460 --> 00:32:55.148
I found no first-party CyberArk
app in the ServiceNow Store.

476
00:32:55.148 --> 00:33:00.150
There is a third-party app from AEYRiX,
called Machine Identity Protection,

477
00:33:00.150 --> 00:33:04.910
released July twenty-ninth, 2026,
that supports the self-hosted edition

478
00:33:04.910 --> 00:33:10.397
through a MID Server and associates
certificates with CMDB configuration items.

479
00:33:10.397 --> 00:33:12.954
That's a third-party
supply-chain component,

480
00:33:12.954 --> 00:33:15.297
so assess it like one before adopting it.

481
00:33:15.297 --> 00:33:16.023
SIEM.

482
00:33:16.023 --> 00:33:20.515
The platform's log server has
a syslog channel with BSD,

483
00:33:20.515 --> 00:33:23.710
CEF or JSON formats, and it supports TLS.

484
00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:26.475
There's a catch worth
knowing before you build:

485
00:33:26.475 --> 00:33:31.714
the collector's server certificate must
carry an IP address subject alternative name

486
00:33:31.714 --> 00:33:36.735
that matches the connection IP, plus the
server authentication extended key usage.

487
00:33:36.735 --> 00:33:40.036
So issue the collector
certificate with an IP address

488
00:33:40.036 --> 00:33:42.897
SAN even if you configure
the collector by name.

489
00:33:42.897 --> 00:33:47.102
Sources: CyberArk 26.1
Configuring Kubernetes discovery;

490
00:33:47.102 --> 00:33:51.482
Syslog channel configuration;
cert-manager documentation,

491
00:33:51.482 --> 00:33:55.249
CyberArk (formerly Venafi)
issuer; ServiceNow Store,

492
00:33:55.249 --> 00:33:59.279
AEYRiX Machine Identity
Protection and Lite editions;

493
00:33:59.279 --> 00:34:04.450
FedRAMP Marketplace products.json;
device-naming research brief.

494
00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:07.512
Part 4: migrate and operate.

495
00:34:07.512 --> 00:34:11.928
First, how the platform build fits
into the Federal Modernization

496
00:34:11.928 --> 00:34:16.424
Workbook's Book 07's six migration
waves, as program design guidance.

497
00:34:16.424 --> 00:34:19.323
Then renewal as public
TLS lifetimes shrink,

498
00:34:19.323 --> 00:34:23.162
and how the platform feeds
monitoring and drift detection.

499
00:34:23.162 --> 00:34:26.287
Then its role in decommissioning AD CS.

500
00:34:26.287 --> 00:34:31.012
And finally the decisions to take, the
questions for the vendor, and next steps.

501
00:34:31.012 --> 00:34:41.880
Sources: Book 07, Chapters 01, 05, 07
and 08; CyberArk 26.1 documentation.

502
00:34:42.630 --> 00:34:45.282
Here's the build mapped onto the Federal

503
00:34:45.282 --> 00:34:48.255
Modernization Workbook's
Book 07's six waves.

504
00:34:48.255 --> 00:34:52.825
This is the platform work that runs
alongside the Cloud PKI waves,

505
00:34:52.825 --> 00:34:54.518
with a gate added to each.

506
00:34:54.518 --> 00:34:56.480
It's program design guidance.

507
00:34:56.480 --> 00:34:59.455
Wave 1, Assess, weeks one to four.

508
00:34:59.455 --> 00:35:04.605
Stand up a two-server test cluster and
its SQL database inside the boundary.

509
00:35:04.605 --> 00:35:09.943
Run read-only network discovery and
import the AD CS templates in scope.

510
00:35:09.943 --> 00:35:13.273
That import needs the
privileged CA account,

511
00:35:13.273 --> 00:35:16.243
so create it now, under the ESC7 controls.

512
00:35:16.243 --> 00:35:21.548
Gate: every certificate without an
issuance record is logged as a finding,

513
00:35:21.548 --> 00:35:24.505
and the CA account is
in the ESC7 baseline.

514
00:35:24.505 --> 00:35:26.243
Wave 2, Foundation.

515
00:35:26.243 --> 00:35:29.886
Build production: at least
two servers per role,

516
00:35:29.886 --> 00:35:32.968
a load balancer, Always
On, keys backed up.

517
00:35:32.968 --> 00:35:37.618
Decide FIPS mode per server,
with ACME on its own servers.

518
00:35:37.618 --> 00:35:40.583
Replace the operational certificates,

519
00:35:40.583 --> 00:35:45.614
set up Entra single sign-on and syslog
over TLS, build the policy tree,

520
00:35:45.614 --> 00:35:49.297
one CA template object per
AD CS template, a CA pool,

521
00:35:49.297 --> 00:35:53.430
the public CA driver and the
password-vault connection.

522
00:35:53.430 --> 00:35:59.042
Gate: a rehearsed restore, a passing
negative test, and IIS hardening applied.

523
00:35:59.042 --> 00:36:00.630
Wave 3, Pilot.

524
00:36:00.630 --> 00:36:06.817
Move pilot servers onto platform renewal,
IIS, Apache, F5, NetScaler and one NPS

525
00:36:06.817 --> 00:36:10.297
server, assign Server
Owners, and pilot ACME,

526
00:36:10.297 --> 00:36:13.680
with autoenrollment left on for rollback.

527
00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:18.505
Wave 4, Scale, moves servers
ring by ring off autoenrollment.

528
00:36:18.505 --> 00:36:21.943
Wave 5 puts all public
TLS on the platform.

529
00:36:21.943 --> 00:36:25.492
Wave 6 retires CAs on
the inventory's evidence.

530
00:36:25.492 --> 00:36:30.953
Sources: Research brief section 9
(program design guidance); Book 07,

531
00:36:30.953 --> 00:36:35.619
Chapter 07 (migration playbook,
waves 1 to 6 and exit gates),

532
00:36:35.619 --> 00:36:40.782
Chapter 04 (issuer decision for
internal server certificates),

533
00:36:40.782 --> 00:36:45.110
Chapter 08 (drift patterns
and decommissioning).

534
00:36:45.860 --> 00:36:48.035
Why renewal automation can't wait.

535
00:36:48.035 --> 00:36:52.958
In April 2025 the CA/Browser Forum
adopted a ballot that cuts the

536
00:36:52.958 --> 00:36:57.973
maximum validity of publicly
trusted TLS certificates in steps.

537
00:36:57.973 --> 00:37:03.534
From March fifteenth, 2026, it's two
hundred days, and that's in force now.

538
00:37:03.534 --> 00:37:07.260
From March fifteenth,
2027, a hundred days.

539
00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:11.185
From March fifteenth,
2029, forty-seven days.

540
00:37:11.185 --> 00:37:16.319
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 07 works out what that means for a

541
00:37:16.319 --> 00:37:21.046
thousand certificates: about seven
renewals every working day today,

542
00:37:21.046 --> 00:37:23.735
fifteen next year, and thirty-one in 2029.

543
00:37:23.735 --> 00:37:27.411
The reuse period for domain
validation falls to ten days,

544
00:37:27.411 --> 00:37:29.835
so validation has to be automated too.

545
00:37:29.835 --> 00:37:33.384
That's why Book 07 Chapter
08 sets one design rule:

546
00:37:33.384 --> 00:37:37.997
no certificate class may depend
on a person remembering a date.

547
00:37:37.997 --> 00:37:42.441
Every class runs through one of
three engines: Intune profiles,

548
00:37:42.441 --> 00:37:46.885
AD CS autoenrollment for domain
controllers, or this platform.

549
00:37:46.885 --> 00:37:49.685
On the platform, renewal works two ways.

550
00:37:49.685 --> 00:37:54.492
Drivers renew the certificate
and push it to the endpoint,

551
00:37:54.492 --> 00:37:57.697
IIS, Apache, F5, NetScaler and the others.

552
00:37:57.697 --> 00:38:00.314
And ACME clients on web listeners renew

553
00:38:00.314 --> 00:38:03.485
themselves against the
platform's ACME service.

554
00:38:03.485 --> 00:38:09.247
When you use the dns-01 challenge, the
authoritative DNS zone becomes the gate

555
00:38:09.247 --> 00:38:15.010
for automated issuance, which is why the
DNS team is consulted on ACME rollout.

556
00:38:15.010 --> 00:38:18.810
The program aligns internal
lifetimes with the schedule too,

557
00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:20.822
as the automation proves itself.

558
00:38:20.822 --> 00:38:27.022
Sources: Book 07, Chapter 01
(CA/Browser Forum Ballot SC-081v3;

559
00:38:27.022 --> 00:38:35.372
Baseline Requirements 2.3.1; renewal
arithmetic) and Chapter 08 (renewal at scale,

560
00:38:35.372 --> 00:38:44.210
ACME where it applies); CyberArk 26.1
provisioning driver and ACME documentation.

561
00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:46.660
Monitoring and drift.

562
00:38:46.660 --> 00:38:52.141
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 07 Chapter 08 collects certificate

563
00:38:52.141 --> 00:38:57.623
signals from many places into one view,
and security operations owns the alerts.

564
00:38:57.623 --> 00:39:03.194
The platform contributes the inventory,
every certificate it knows with its owner,

565
00:39:03.194 --> 00:39:08.361
expiry and renewal status, and sends its
events to the SIEM over syslog with TLS.

566
00:39:08.361 --> 00:39:12.410
Four of Chapter 08's drift patterns
are the platform's to catch.

567
00:39:12.410 --> 00:39:16.370
Expiring with no renewal
path: a certificate within two

568
00:39:16.370 --> 00:39:20.072
renewal windows of expiry
with no engine bound to it.

569
00:39:20.072 --> 00:39:25.776
Issuance outside policy: a discovered
certificate with no enrollment record, or,

570
00:39:25.776 --> 00:39:29.096
from Part 2, event 4887
showing the platform's CA

571
00:39:29.096 --> 00:39:33.011
account received a certificate
outside its templates.

572
00:39:33.011 --> 00:39:35.905
CRLs near their next
update or served stale:

573
00:39:35.905 --> 00:39:40.087
the platform's Certificate
Revocation Monitoring component

574
00:39:40.087 --> 00:39:44.510
checks CRL distribution points and
OCSP endpoints, at least daily.

575
00:39:44.510 --> 00:39:49.964
And trust store drift: the program's guidance
is to keep the platform's Roots tree

576
00:39:49.964 --> 00:39:52.360
limited to approved trust anchors,

577
00:39:52.360 --> 00:39:55.748
which is the SC-17 part b
question an assessor asks.

578
00:39:55.748 --> 00:39:57.460
And persist the evidence.

579
00:39:57.460 --> 00:40:00.619
In the program's model the drift
checks run at least weekly,

580
00:40:00.619 --> 00:40:03.910
and their outputs are committed
to the evidence repository.

581
00:40:03.910 --> 00:40:08.028
History matters more than any single
run, because an assessor asks

582
00:40:08.028 --> 00:40:11.697
how long a finding stayed open,
not only whether it's open now.

583
00:40:11.697 --> 00:40:18.079
Sources: Book 07, Chapter 05 (security
operations role) and Chapter 08 (monitoring,

584
00:40:18.079 --> 00:40:21.367
drift patterns, persisting the evidence);

585
00:40:21.367 --> 00:40:25.139
CyberArk 26.1 Installation
and Upgrade Guide p.

586
00:40:25.139 --> 00:40:28.330
89 (Certificate Revocation Monitoring);

587
00:40:28.330 --> 00:40:33.330
Syslog channel configuration;
research brief section 11.

588
00:40:34.080 --> 00:40:38.882
Decommissioning is the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 07's Wave 6,

589
00:40:38.882 --> 00:40:41.480
and the platform has a defined part in it.

590
00:40:41.480 --> 00:40:43.864
The gate comes first: cutover complete,

591
00:40:43.864 --> 00:40:47.365
the thirty-day rollback window
passed with no incidents,

592
00:40:47.365 --> 00:40:52.580
and every retained certificate class
documented and running on the CAs that remain.

593
00:40:52.580 --> 00:40:57.260
Step one of Book 07's procedure is to
confirm nothing depends on the CA,

594
00:40:57.260 --> 00:41:02.680
and the platform's inventory is the main
evidence, together with the CA database.

595
00:41:02.680 --> 00:41:07.967
It has to show that no unexpired certificate
in a migrated class is still in use.

596
00:41:07.967 --> 00:41:11.729
Steps two to five retire the
CA itself: stop new issuance,

597
00:41:11.729 --> 00:41:17.045
back up and archive the database and
key, revoke outstanding certificates for

598
00:41:17.045 --> 00:41:22.605
cessation of operation and publish the
final CRL, and uninstall the role services.

599
00:41:22.605 --> 00:41:26.784
Before you stop issuance,
repoint any CyberArk CA template

600
00:41:26.784 --> 00:41:31.218
objects that used this CA to a
retained CA, so renewals don't fail.

601
00:41:31.218 --> 00:41:34.603
Step six cleans up trust:
the Entra trust store,

602
00:41:34.603 --> 00:41:40.393
Intune trusted certificate profiles,
NTAuth, and the CA object in the platform.

603
00:41:40.393 --> 00:41:43.491
Retiring that CA object is
the platform owner's task,

604
00:41:43.491 --> 00:41:46.517
and the decommission record
should say it was done.

605
00:41:46.517 --> 00:41:50.479
Steps seven to nine keep the
CRL and AIA endpoints serving

606
00:41:50.479 --> 00:41:54.442
until the last certificate
expires, and commit the record.

607
00:41:54.442 --> 00:41:56.816
After Active Directory retires,

608
00:41:56.816 --> 00:42:02.705
Book 07 moves server issuance to a standalone
AD CS issuing CA under the same root,

609
00:42:02.705 --> 00:42:07.892
still driven by the platform, which
supports standalone Microsoft CAs.

610
00:42:07.892 --> 00:42:13.004
Template policy then lives in the platform,
and there's no new root to distribute.

611
00:42:13.004 --> 00:42:18.729
Sources: Book 07, Chapter 08
(Decommissioning AD CS, steps 1 to 9),

612
00:42:18.729 --> 00:42:23.651
Chapter 07 (Wave 6), Chapter
04 (after Active Directory);

613
00:42:23.651 --> 00:42:30.176
CyberArk 26.1 Microsoft ADCS
Enterprise and Standalone CA template;

614
00:42:30.176 --> 00:42:35.120
Microsoft Learn, Manage
certificate templates.

615
00:42:35.870 --> 00:42:39.845
To close: decisions, vendor
asks, and next steps.

616
00:42:39.845 --> 00:42:41.320
The decisions to take.

617
00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:46.132
Which servers run in FIPS mode, and
which separate servers run ACME.

618
00:42:46.132 --> 00:42:50.706
Whether database secrets are protected
by an HSM or by the software key,

619
00:42:50.706 --> 00:42:53.419
and whether SQL is Enterprise or Standard.

620
00:42:53.419 --> 00:42:58.232
Console sign-in through Entra single sign-on
with certificate-based authentication.

621
00:42:58.232 --> 00:43:01.983
The public CA path, if the
agency uses Entrust today.

622
00:43:01.983 --> 00:43:05.138
Whether to build the Cloud
PKI feed on a beta API,

623
00:43:05.138 --> 00:43:07.808
and whether to adopt the ServiceNow app.

624
00:43:07.808 --> 00:43:12.195
Ask CyberArk, in writing: which
statement governs ACME and FIPS;

625
00:43:12.195 --> 00:43:17.174
whether AD CS on Windows Server 2025
is supported, given the stale table;

626
00:43:17.174 --> 00:43:20.380
which cryptographic
module the product uses,

627
00:43:20.380 --> 00:43:25.864
and whether any CMVP certificate covers
it; whether the Key Vault driver works

628
00:43:25.864 --> 00:43:31.433
with Azure Government; and whether PIV or
CAC console login is supported as such.

629
00:43:31.433 --> 00:43:36.286
Next steps: stand up the Wave 1
test cluster, create the CA account

630
00:43:36.286 --> 00:43:41.232
under the ESC7 controls, and lab-test
allowed and denied requests.

631
00:43:41.232 --> 00:43:48.489
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
07 has been updated in Chapters 03 and 05

632
00:43:48.489 --> 00:43:53.222
for the ESC7 caveat, Chapter
04 for Cloud PKI reporting,

633
00:43:53.222 --> 00:43:55.957
and Chapter 08 for ACME and FIPS.

634
00:43:55.957 --> 00:44:00.757
The program's recommendation is
plain: CyberArk Certificate Manager,

635
00:44:00.757 --> 00:44:04.646
Self-Hosted, is the right
platform for Book 07's lifecycle.

636
00:44:04.646 --> 00:44:08.892
It's the agency's existing
machine-identity vendor, it covers every

637
00:44:08.892 --> 00:44:13.370
issuer the design assigns to it, and
its risks are known and manageable.

638
00:44:13.370 --> 00:44:15.434
The backup slides list the risks,

639
00:44:15.434 --> 00:44:18.720
the assessor evidence and
what couldn't be verified.

640
00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:21.760
This is the program's
research, under review,

641
00:44:21.760 --> 00:44:24.632
not an endorsement by any federal agency.

642
00:44:24.632 --> 00:44:25.394
Thank you.

643
00:44:25.394 --> 00:44:33.662
